{"name":"Amber Coast Tide","hex":"#3aa098","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"amber","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ambergris, the waxy product of the sperm whale. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A hard, generally yellow to brown translucent or transparent fossil resin from extinct coniferous trees of the pine genus, used for jewellery, decoration and later dissolved as a binder in varnishes. One variety, blue amber, appears blue rather than yellow under direct sunlight."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a brownish yellow colour, like that of most amber."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amber"},{"term":"coast","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The edge of the land where it meets an ocean, sea, gulf, bay, or large lake. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The side or edge of something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To glide along without adding energy; to allow a vehicle to continue moving forward after disengaging the engine or ceasing to apply motive power.","To sail along a coast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coast"},{"term":"tide","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The daily fluctuation in the level of the sea caused by the gravitational influence of the moon and the sun.","The associated flow of water."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause to float with the tide; to drive or carry with the tide or stream.","To carry over or through a problem or difficulty."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tide"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amber Coast","blocks":[{"text":"The Amber Coast is a coastal strip of the Baltic Sea in the northwest of Kaliningrad. In this area amber has been excavated since the mid-19th century and up to today in open-pit mining. Two deposits – Palmnikenskoe and Primorskoe, containing 80% of world amber reserves, were found near Yantarny on the Western coast of the Sambia Peninsula in 1948-1951's."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/KaliningradOB.png/330px-KaliningradOB.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber_Coast","wikidata":"Q592227"},{"title":"Amber","blocks":[{"text":"Amber is fossilized tree resin. It has been appreciated for its color and natural beauty since Neolithic times, and worked as a gemstone since classical antiquity. Amber is used in jewelry and as a healing agent in folk medicine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Amber2.jpg/330px-Amber2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber","wikidata":"Q25381"},{"title":"Coast","blocks":[{"text":"A coast is the land next to the sea or the line that forms the boundary between the land and the ocean or a lake. Coasts are influenced by the topography of the surrounding landscape and by aquatic erosion, such as that caused by waves. The geological composition of rock and soil dictates the type of shore that is created. Earth has about 620,000.0 km (385,250.1 mi) of coastline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg/330px-Spring_Lake%2C_New_Jersey_Beach_at_Sunrise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast","wikidata":"Q93352"},{"title":"Tide","blocks":[{"text":"Tides are the periodic rise and fall of sea level resulting from the differential gravitational forces exerted primarily by the Moon and the Sun, combined with inertial effects associated with the Earth–Moon system’s orbital motion and the Earth's rotation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Tide_overview.svg/330px-Tide_overview.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tide","wikidata":"Q23384"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/KaliningradOB.png/330px-KaliningradOB.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/KaliningradOB.png/330px-KaliningradOB.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Sambia Peninsula, Bay of Gdańsk and Vistula Lagoon; the area of the \"Amber Coast\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKaliningradOB.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Pribrezhnyje_yantarnyje_razrabotki.jpg/500px-Pribrezhnyje_yantarnyje_razrabotki.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Pribrezhnyje_yantarnyje_razrabotki.jpg/500px-Pribrezhnyje_yantarnyje_razrabotki.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Open-pit mining near Jantarny (Sambia Peninsula, Kaliningrad Oblast, Russia)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APribrezhnyje_yantarnyje_razrabotki.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Colours_of_Baltic_Amber.jpg/500px-Colours_of_Baltic_Amber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Colours_of_Baltic_Amber.jpg/500px-Colours_of_Baltic_Amber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different colours of Baltic amber.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColours_of_Baltic_Amber.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Amber2.jpg/500px-Amber2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Amber2.jpg/1280px-Amber2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn